<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
	The app's privacy manifest (Milestone 9, docs/25-milestone-9-plan.md section
	C; the answers it encodes are written out in docs/STORE.md).

	Two things live in here. The first three keys are the truthful, and very
	short, answer to "what does this app do with people's data": nothing. No
	ads, no IAP, no analytics SDK, no network calls at all — a standing decision
	(D6) and not an oversight. The save is `localStorage` on the device
	(src/core/save.ts) and never leaves it.

	The fourth key, NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes, is the different question Apple
	asks: which "required reason" APIs does the binary reach, and why. Capacitor
	ships its own manifests inside the framework (Capacitor.framework and the
	Cordova compatibility framework) and both declare an empty list, so anything
	this app's process touches has to be declared here. What it touches is
	WebKit's storage: WKWebView backs `localStorage` with files in the app
	container and with app-scoped user defaults, and it looks at their metadata
	and at free space while managing them. That is where all three entries below
	come from — not from our own Swift, of which there is almost none.

	Deliberately *not* declared: NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime and
	NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryActiveKeyboards. Nothing in the app or its five
	plugins reads either; the game's own clocks are `performance.now()` and the
	device date used for the daily streak, which are not required-reason APIs.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- No tracking, and so no tracking domains to list. -->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<!-- Nothing is collected, so there is nothing to describe. -->
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<!--
				CA92.1: read and write user defaults that only this app can see.
				WKWebView keeps per-app WebKit preferences there, and nothing we
				write is shared with another app or an App Group.
			-->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!--
				C617.1: timestamps of files inside the app's own container.
				WebKit stats its storage files, and Capacitor's asset handler
				reads the size of the bundled web files to answer range requests
				(WebViewAssetHandler.swift). Both are inside the container; none
				of it is sent anywhere.
			-->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!--
				E174.1: check that there is space before writing. WebKit asks
				before it grows the storage quota that `localStorage` writes
				into, which is every time the game saves.
			-->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryDiskSpace</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>E174.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
